\title{Definitions as translations}
\import{prelude}

\p{Elements of a record type\code{\kw{sig} ... \kw{end}} are introduced by writing \code{\kw{struct} ... \kw{end}} and providing an element for all its fields. For example:}

\pre{%
\kw{theory} PersonName := \kw{sig}
  first-name : String
  last-name : String
\kw{end}

\kw{def} charlies-name : PersonName := \kw{struct}
  first-name := "Charlie"
  last-name := "Bucket"
\kw{end}
}

\p{When using \code{\kw{struct}}, one must give all the fields of the original record type in the same order as they were originally defined.}

\p{As with queries, the real power comes when definitions may take arguments, allowing us to transform a model of one theory into a model of another.}

\pre{%
\kw{def} name-mixup (m : PersonName) : PersonName := \kw{struct}
  first-name := m.last-name
  last-name := m.first-name
\kw{end}
}


\pre{%
\kw{theory} Relatives := \kw{sig}
  Ancestor : Set
  Descendant : Set
\kw{end}

\kw{def} relatives-of (m : Family) (p : m.Person) : Relatives := \kw{struct}
  Ancestor := \kw{sig}
    person : m.Person
    m.is-descendant-of p person
  \kw{end}
  Descendant := \kw{sig}
    person : m.Person
    m.is-descendant-of person p
  \kw{end}
\kw{end}
}

\p{\mvrnote{update} Intuitively, this assigns \code{naughty} to the set of children naughty in that year, and similarly with \code{nice}. The \code{\kw{sig}} syntax is just as in [[001H]], using the curly-brace inline syntax. And \code{m.list y .naughty child} is just a sequence of function applications and field projection. Walking through the types, we have \code{m.list : m.person -> SantaListOver m.person}, so \code{m .list y : SantaListOver m.person} and then \code{m.list y .naughty : m.person -> Prop} and finally \code{m.list y .naughty child : Prop}.}


\p{Elements of a function type \code{(x : A) -> B} are introduced using the lambda syntax \code{x => a} (similar to anonymous functions in JavaScript).}

\p{As an example, we can produce the graph of a function, which is a binary relation on the domain and codomain.}

\pre{%
\kw{def} graph (X : Set) (Y : Set) (f : X -> Y) : X -> Y -> Prop :=
  x => y => f x = y
}

\p{We can also now make non-trivial translations of theories that involve function types. For instance, we can translate a boat ride into one that advances two stops at a time.}

\pre{%
\kw{def} double-time (m : BoatRide) : BoatRide := \kw{struct}
  Stop := m.Stop
  next := x => m.next (m.next x)
\kw{end}
}
